@meta
id: 03-11
title: What the arrow keys actually cost
chapter: 03
type: lesson
skills: insert-A
par: 28
minutes: 5
vscode: five clicks, or five End presses, and five commas
open: timeouts.ts

@brief
Five entries in this object are missing their trailing commas. Add all
five.

There is nothing new here. You could have done this after lesson one. The
reason it is a lesson is that it is the first job in the dojo big enough
to show you the price of the habit you brought with you from VS Code:
enter insert mode once, then steer with the arrow keys and Home and End
until the file is right.

That route finishes the job. The dojo counts your keystrokes, so it will
also tell you exactly what it cost. Do it the vim way first, then read the
solution for the numbers.

@keys
| `A` | append at the end of the line |
| `j` | down one line |
| `Esc` | out |

@hints
One trip into insert mode per line, not one trip for the file. The
sequence for a line is three keys, and moving to the next line is one
more.
--
`j` `A` `,` `Esc`, five times over. Your fingers should settle into it by
the third line.
--
The cursor starts on line one, so the first thing you press is `j`, not
`A`.

@solution
    j A , <Esc>   j A , <Esc>   j A , <Esc>   j A , <Esc>   j A , <Esc>   :wq<Enter>

Twenty-four keystrokes. Par is 28.

Now the same job done by staying in insert mode and arrowing around, which
is what nearly everyone coming from VS Code does for their first month.
Press `i` once, then Down to the next line, Right along it to the end,
type a comma, Down, Right, comma, and so on. Both routes produce a file
that passes. These are the real measured counts:

| route | keystrokes |
|---|---|
| `j A , Esc`, five times | 24 |
| insert once, Down and End | 41 |
| insert once, arrows only | 92 |

Ninety-two against a par of 28. More than three times the cost for the
same five commas, and that is with the arrow route played well — it
assumes you never overshoot a line and never have to come back.

The arithmetic behind it is worth knowing, because it is not a metaphor.
Vim records a Down arrow as three bytes, and `j` as one. Every arrow press
you make is literally three times the keystroke of the normal-mode key
that does the same thing. A key that is one third the cost, used hundreds
of times a day, is most of the reason vim users look fast.

And the byte count is the smaller half of the argument. The larger half:
while you are arrowing around inside insert mode, none of vim exists. No
counts, no word motions, no operators, no repeat, no undo granularity —
every chapter after this one is switched off, because all of it lives in
normal mode. Insert mode is a room with one tool in it.

So: leave insert mode the moment you stop typing. Not when you have
finished the file — when you have finished the words. `Esc` is free, and
everything good is on the other side of it.

